<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naqada III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3325–3000 BCE</span> · Also known as Protodynastic Period</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> III is the last archaeological phase of the Naqada culture of ancient Egyptian prehistory, dated depending on the sources to approximately 3325 to 3000 BCE or c. 3350 to c. 2920 BCE. Often described as the Protodynastic Period, it was the time when state formation became highly visible, with named kings, written in serekhs, heading powerful polities; its later part is associated with Dynasty 0. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> was reduced to the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a>, Naqada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Nekhen</a>, and the Thinite kings, buried at Abydos, came to rule all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Egyptian language was first recorded in hieroglyphs in this period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naqada-iii.jpg" alt="Naqada III" width="330" height="749" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heidi Kontkanen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_figurine_from_Tell_el-Farkha._Late_Predynastic_period_(Naqada_IIIB,_c._3200–3000_BCE)._Egyptian_Museum_(Cairo)._30cm_height_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
